WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
So, on the channel we spend a lot of time talking about communications theories, language use, and the structure of ideas,

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.720
so here's an opportunity to put all that theory into practice.

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.460
Contact your representatives.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:19.580
[MUSIC]

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.680
Now this is advice that you'll find embedded in every political call to action.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:30.120
Whenever a journalist asks an activist, "What can people do?", the top of the list is always "Contact your representatvies".

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:35.740
So this is going to be a concrete overview of what that means, and how you should do it.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.580
Now, I'm going to try to make this generalized for any legislative democratic system

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:48.280
but it will still be ever so slightly slanted towards the United States owing to their...recent turn of events.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:55.920
First, what do you want? Always have a clear goal. Your communication needs to be focused and ask a specific question.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:02.880
And keep it contained: one call, one subject. If there's 20 things you want to say, get ready to write 20 letters.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.440
What you're really looking for is a strong call to action that requires some kind of feedback.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:12.700
You are contacting your rep and there is something you want. Can they do it?

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:19.560
Even when you're calling to express general dissatisfaction, you want to structure it in a way that asks for a direct response.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:28.240
The reason for that is when you get someone to formulate a response, you are getting them to come up with verifiable claims, promises, or statements of intent

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.360
to which they should be held accountable.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.860
If you are holding them to account, then you're asking them to reconcile promises with reality.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:41.940
And what you're actually looking for is uncertainty, a point where the representative's platform is weak or disconnected,

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.660
because that's a gap for negotiation.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:51.520
That's the path to saying, "Hey, here's a better, stronger, more just, more moral, more compassionate platform.

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.520
Here's the human impact, here's the human cost, and here's what you can do about it."

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.040
Second, what level of government are you looking to contact?

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.100
This is going to have a dramatic impact on your plan.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.500
The more local you aim your communications, the easier it is to get an ear.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:12.480
There's a couple of reasons for this. The obvious is that representative systems are pyramid shaped.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.040
The people towards the top represent a lot more individuals than the people towards the bottom,

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.840
so there's simply more people who want their time.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:26.700
Tied to that, the people towards the top have a lot more power, so getting their attention has potentially greater payoffs.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:33.180
The more local you get--state or provincial, municipality or county--the easier it is to get someone's ear.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:40.460
Not to say it's a guarantee, they're still busy people. Being an alderman in a mid-size city is still a full-time job.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:47.620
But they and their assistants won't be getting the same sheer volume of communication that a congressman's office will be.

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:53.400
But this is going to be the biggest question to answer, because it's going to inform just about everything about your strategy.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:59.840
What methods of communication do you use? How do you time it? How is it structured? Do you coordinate with others? And so on.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.500
Third, have you done your research?

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:08.260
What do you know about your representatives and their stance on various subjects? What's their voting record look like?

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:13.720
Now, a lot of this stuff might be online, available in summary from various stakeholders like activist groups,

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.920
and you should definitely check their website or Twitter feed or Facebook for some official platform.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:23.880
but this is also a perfectly valid thing to open up lines of communications with.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.960
Especially for phone calls, it can be good practice.

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:33.720
You're calling your representative's office because you want to know how they voted on a given bill and you're having trouble finding it online.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:38.660
Or you read their platform, but some statements were vague and you want clarification.

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:42.020
Because keep in mind that nothing will undermine your communication faster

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:49.560
than an angry phone call where a staffer kindly explains that Representative Clark did, in fact, vote No.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:55.340
Just the same as your reps lose credibility when it's clear, in your eyes, that they don't know what they're talking about,

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.820
the same will happen to you if it's obvious that you haven't done your homework.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.300
Now, generally speaking, you want to stick to your representatives

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:07.840
because staffers are conditioned to discard contact from non-constituents out of hand,

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.460
but this isn't a hard and fast rule.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:16.220
As part of the government, representatives have additional roles and responsibilities on various committees and boards,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:21.300
and if you have a concern relevant to those roles, then yes, you should contact them.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.460
Fourth, what's the relationship like?

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:29.100
Is it adversarial? This means that you don't agree with the representatives politics, you don't agree with their policies,

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.740
or they're doing something and you want them to do the opposite instead.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:37.000
The degree to which the relationship is supportive or adversarial is huge because

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:41.720
the further you need to persuade someone to move, the greater an impact you need to make.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:47.040
And while we're here, "contact your representatives" doesn't just mean "convince them to oppose something",

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.400
it also means, "let them know if they're doing a good job".

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.140
Let them know you support them, and contact them to ask if there's anything you can do to help.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:05:04.240
Because if there is something you can do to help, if there's an open hearing you can attend, if there's a rally you can volunteer for, if there's a fund you can donate to,

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.520
they're going to know that.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:12.840
Also, even if you know that the relationship is probably adversarial, always open your communication lines with the assumption that they're not.

00:05:12.860 --> 00:05:20.140
Assume good faith. If they're making poor decisions, then assume they made a mistake or they were uninformed of the consequences.

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:24.360
This is particularly important when it comes to phone calls and talking with their staff.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:30.140
Don't be mean. Be firm, be angry if you need to be, but don't be mean.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:34.320
Fifth, what channels of communication are you looking to use?

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:41.140
Ranked in order of impact, the options are face-to-face, phone calls, physical mail,  e-mail, and social media.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:46.160
Physical mail, e-mail, and social media are all text based, and they're all discretionary,

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.440
meaning the recipient can engage with them at their own pace on their own time,

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:56.060
and you can write them from home, proofread, and really put a lot of work into crafting a very tight message.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:06:02.800
Social media is cheap, it's instant, and it's public. Those attributes make it useful, but also make it popular,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.760
meaning that competition for attention, for mental bandwidth, is very high.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.320
Social media also tends to be poor at communicating long, complex ideas.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.820
This has to do with user interfaces, character limits, and the reader's state of mind while engaging with the service.

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:20.140
Basically, the medium is the message,

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:27.140
so with that in mind, social media tends to be best at communicating overall tone rather than specific ideas

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:30.560
unless those ideas are very simple and very short.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.940
The main strength of social media is the public aspect.

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:39.400
Building momentum, getting others to join in, getting the attention of media, and creating public pressure.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:45.780
E-mail is also cheap and instant, but it's a lot better suited to communicating longer ideas than social media.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.640
It's also really easy to ignore, and really easy to sort and filter.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:57.360
The busier an office is, the more inclined they're going to be to scan e-mails for general subject and respond with a form letter.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:04.720
Now physical mail. In many places elected representatives are legally obligated to read every piece of mail that they get.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:10.540
In practical terms, that usually means having their staff read it for them and sum it up or select a few exemplars,

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:13.380
but physical mail is still incredibly valuable.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:18.900
The only downsides are that it's not instant, it is personally inconvenient, and it isn't free.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:24.540
But here's the thing to keep in mind, and we're going to talk about phone calls in a second, which are super powerful,

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:28.900
but there's a particular strength of physical letters that nothing else can match.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.220
And it's the role as a prop.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:37.280
See, politics is entirely about persuasion, whether that be through force, money, rhetoric, or reason.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:43.240
When your representatives go to the floor, their job is to try and persuade everyone else in the room to agree with them,

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:47.280
which means that there is a performative aspect to their job.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:51.500
Think to yourself how many times you've seen a clip of a representative on the floor saying:

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:58.800
"Madam Speaker, Joe Beerdrinker from My Writing wrote me this letter, and in this letter he says..."

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.680
Writing to your representatives is literally an opportunity to put your words into their mouths.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:15.460
The value of this is hard to overstate. It's a chance to have a direct impact on the way that an issue is framed, the language that is used, and the lens that it is projected through.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:20.480
Think of how many issues today, from gender and sexuality to mental health, classism, race,

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:27.440
that are defined almost entirely by the language that people use, because that language sets the frame.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:33.780
How many damaging policies are in place right now because of phrases like "Boys will be boys"?

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:39.480
Contacting your reps, letters in particular, is an opportunity to shape language.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:45.320
Now, as a matter of protocol, you always want to prioritize the local office over the federal office,

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:47.240
and this goes for phone calls as well.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:53.060
Phone calls are the single most effective channel of communication you have, without leaving your home.

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:56.800
Unlike written communication, phone calls aren't discretionary.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.440
If you're on the phone right now, their staff needs to talk to you right now.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:07.380
They can't skim your phone call for subject and tone at 9 PM while watching Stranger Things,

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:10.540
you have their time, right now.

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:16.540
Again, the reality is that you're more likely to talk to their staff than to them personally, especially at the federal level.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:25.380
So the strategy, the game of persuasion that you're playing, is to impact the staff enough that the message gets passed on clearly and actionably.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:28.060
And we'll come back to this later when we talk about strategy.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:32.720
Now, as someone who hates making phone calls, yes, they can be intimidating.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:40.220
Don't be ashamed to practice with a friend first, to roleplay, to make notes, or even write out your question or statement in advance.

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:45.560
You want to make a human connection, but you don't want your message to get lost or to be unclear.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:48.840
Face-to-face meetings basically mean town hall meetings,

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:55.380
and town hall meetings are, generally, woefully under-attended by the same couple dozen people every single time.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:59.920
This is good news. You can be one of those couple dozen people.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:03.460
And if you show up with four, five, ten, twenty friends,

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:08.060
if you all prepare your questions beforehand, you can be a block that they can't ignore.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:15.060
If you've ever screamed at a TV interviewer who just let a softball non-answer slide, town halls are your chance to fix that.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.400
Finally, what's your strategy?

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.520
One of the most valuable things you can do is get a group together,

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:25.080
whether that means coordinating with a bunch of friends to send letters or phone calls at the same time,

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:27.820
or joining an advocacy group and coordinating with them,

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:31.620
the more voices you can add together, the more impactful the message will be.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:36.340
If you pack a town hall meeting, then yes, your issues are going to be discussed.

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:41.040
If you tie up their staff on the phone all day, if that becomes a day where nothing else got done

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:47.320
because they were answering calls from constituents for six straight hours? Yeah, there's going to be follow-up.

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:52.560
Now, with that though, and this is going back to letter writing, be careful with form letters.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:59.680
Advocacy groups will typically provide sample letters, and sending a form letter is better than sending nothing,

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:04.220
but humans are built for pattern recognition, so there's a very fine line here.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:10.120
Common language, like specific phrasing, is a way to set the way that your concerns are framed.

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:16.460
If a staffer reads the same phrase over and over again, they're going to fall back on that phrasing when they pass the message along.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:20.920
But if it's too similar? If it's clearly the exact same letter?

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:24.860
Well, then it just gets added to the stack. It doesn't have the same impact.

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:31.960
As an interjection, I'm stressing the value of acting as part of a group, but don't ever let the lack of a group stop you.

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:36.460
Because here's the thing: being politically active is a thing that happens over time.

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:42.620
Just because you don't have a group now, doesn't mean you won't have one two months from now, a year from now,

00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:46.420
as you do more research, as you attend town halls, as you meet other people.

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:54.020
But if you never write a letter? If you never make a phone call? If you never go to a town hall? If you never start researching?

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:58.640
Well, let's just say it's unlikely that you'll just stumble into it in the future.

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:03.060
Activism is a skill, and like any other skill, it's built over time.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:07.640
So don't just write or call once. Do it repeatedly, even if it's on the same issue.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:11.460
If they're refusing to answer a reasonable policy question, stick with it.

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:17.460
Yes, I'd like to believe that most people have representatives that are ultimately rational and amenable to change,

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:22.000
but there will be adversarial relationships, if not outright hostile ones.

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:24.240
But repeat yourself if necessary.

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:32.780
Don't worry about being obnoxious, or rather, don't let the fear that you will be perceived as obnoxious stop you from writing or calling.

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:39.620
To a point, you do want to be a thorn in their side, because even when the relationship is hostile, volume matters.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:44.180
One of the other things that should stand out at this point is the importance of a representative's staff.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:49.180
They are part of the inner circle. They feed ideas and words and materials directly to them,

00:12:49.180 --> 00:12:52.440
summarizing the vast bulk of information that comes in every day.

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:55.720
Their job is to interface with the constituency.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:58.860
Get to know them. Meet them. Persuade them.

00:12:58.860 --> 00:13:02.960
Strategy should also take a look at which levels of government you want to focus on.

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:11.860
In an ideal world, everyone has the time and energy to research, contact, and recontact their municipal, state, and federal representatives about every issue,

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:17.720
but...it's exhausting. It's a lot of information. It's a lot of time. It's a lot of energy.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:21.940
The advice here changes a lot based on what the issue is that you're concerned about,

00:13:21.940 --> 00:13:28.220
so you want to find the level where you can make the most tangible impact with the energy that you have available.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:34.960
A lot of times that energy is actually going to be better directed a lot lower, at the civic or state level rather than federal.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:40.440
See, even if the federal government sets the pace for policies surrounding, say, policing,

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:44.700
it's the civic leaders who actually meet with the police officers who live and work in your neighborhoods,

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:47.740
who tell the police chiefs what the citizens are saying.

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:56.060
It's a lot easier to get your city to change how it views mental health crises and whether 911 should send police or EMT's to those calls,

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:59.360
then it is to change the entire nation's stance.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:06.860
And that flows upwards. Local successes become support for state programs. State successes become the case studies for federal policy.

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:11.380
It's a slow, grinding process, yes, but it is possible.

00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:15.040
So...contact your representatives.

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:16.640
Thank you for watching.

